      "finalUrl": "https://eivimeycook.github.io/TADA-Wiki/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "Welcome | The TADA-Wiki",
      "pageDescription": "A wiki on sharing analytical code, built around the TADA guidelines (Transferable, Available, Documented, Annotated). Other chapters cover data-sharing basics, metadata, licences, the SORTEE Guidelines for Data and Code Quality Control in Ecology and Evolutionary Biology, and code review.",
      "evidenceText": "Welcome | The TADA-Wiki Welcome Best practice for code sharing Code that runs for someone else, not just for you Much of the code behind published papers is never shared, and what is shared often won’t run. This wiki introduces four principles for sharing analytical code so that others can understand, run and reuse it: make your code T ransferable, A vailable, D ocumented and A nnotated. New to sharing code? Sharing code means making the scripts behind your paper publicly available and depositing them in a permanent repository. Where possible, archive the code alongside your data, so that readers can access, understand and rerun your analysis. By the end of this wiki you will have: a project folder holding your data, scripts and a short README that explains them; code that runs on someone else’s computer , not just yours; an archived copy of that folder in a repository such as Zenodo, with a DOI (a persistent identifier you can cite in your paper). You do not need to be an experienced programmer. If you can write and run an R or Python script, you can follow this wiki. You do not need Git, Docker or unit testing either; where they help, they are mentioned as optional. Submitting soon? Go straight to Start here . It is a checklist you can work through with your project open, and most items link to a fuller explanation if you need one. Jump in Start here The pre-submission checklist, a project layout that works and the three most common causes of failure. TADA at a glance Why TADA exists, the evidence behind it and how it maps onto FAIR. Write a README or pick a licence A field-by-field README, a worked example and a guide to code and data licences. Choose a repository Zenodo, Figshare, Dryad and others compared, and why GitHub is not an archive. What a data editor will check The six-stage SORTEE guidelines for data and code quality control. Set up a code review Why, when and how to have a colleague run and check your code. The four principles T Transferable Runs on someone else’s computer, not just yours. A Available Archived in a permanent repository, with a DOI (a persistent identifier). D Documented A README (a guide to your files) and a licence (what others may do with them). A Annotated Comments that explain what the code does and why. Words you will meet A few terms come up on almost every page. Each has a fuller entry in the Glossary . Term What it means Repository A service for archiving data and code for the long term, such as Zenodo, Figshare or Dryad. This is different from a GitHub repository, which is generally used to host and manage working code. DOI Digital Object Identifier: a persistent identifier for a deposit, such as 10.5281/zenodo.1234567 . The https://doi.org/ form is its resolvable link, which you cite like a paper. Immutable Cannot be changed or deleted once published. An update creates a new version rather than overwriting the old one. README A plain-text file at the top of your project that says what it is, what each file does and how to run it. Licence A statement of what others may legally do with your code or data. Without one, others cannot lawfully reuse it. Relative path A file path written from your project folder, such as data/raw/counts.csv , so it can work across different computers. Data editor A journal editor who checks the data and code archived alongside a manuscript while it is under review. Also in the wiki Data & code sharing · Further reading · Glossary · Contribute · References Want the background first? Read About this wiki : why it exists, who it is for and where the guidance comes from. About this wiki",
